## For the agent — instructions

You are conducting a manual security review of the codebase in the current working directory. Work through the phases below in order. Do the work — do not summarise this prompt back to the user.

## Phase 1 — Map the codebase

Before reviewing anything, build a mental model. Capture:

- **Stack**: language, framework + version, runtime version (note if EOL).
- **Dependencies**: read the lockfiles (`Gemfile.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `go.sum`, `requirements.txt`, `Cargo.lock`, `pom.xml`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, etc.). Note versions of high-risk libraries (auth, SSO, parsers, serialisers, web servers, image processors).
- **Entry points**: HTTP routes/controllers, WebSocket handlers, queue consumers, scheduled jobs, webhooks, CLI tools.
- **Trust boundaries**: public vs. authenticated vs. admin surfaces. Identify where each lives in the code.
- **Auth & sessions**: how users authenticate, how sessions are stored, what roles exist, where role checks are enforced.
- **Data stores**: ORMs in use; locations of raw SQL or query-string interpolation.
- **External calls**: HTTP clients, AWS/GCP SDKs, AI/LLM clients, payment gateways. Note plaintext HTTP, follow-redirect middleware, SSRF-prone URL inputs.
- **File / data handling**: uploads, image processing, XML/YAML/CSV/ZIP parsing, deserialisation sinks (`Marshal`, `pickle`, `ObjectInputStream`, `YAML.load`, etc.).
- **Existing security signals**: `brakeman.ignore`, `.snyk`, `SECURITY.md`, `.github/dependabot.yml`, prior CHANGELOG entries flagged "security." Read these first — they record what's already been triaged.

Skip vendored / generated trees (`node_modules`, `vendor/`, `dist/`, `build/`, `.next/`) unless a specific finding requires them.

Do not review what you have not mapped.

## Phase 2 — Review

Work breadth-first across the categories below. For every finding, cite `file:line` (or a line range). If you cannot cite the exact location, omit the finding.

| Category | What to look for |
|---|---|
| **dependencies** | EOL runtimes/frameworks, libraries with widely-known CVEs at the pinned version, abandoned packages. Cross-reference your knowledge of CVE history; if uncertain, list the version and recommend `bundler-audit` / `npm audit` / equivalent. |
| **authentication** | Password hashing (bcrypt/argon2 vs. unsalted), MFA, SSO (SAML signature validation, OIDC nonce/state), session lifetime, "remember me" tokens, password reset flows, persistence tokens. |
| **authorization** | Role checks present on every privileged action (especially destructive admin endpoints), IDOR (resource lookups by user-supplied ID without ownership check), mass assignment via `permit`, role downgrade / offboarding paths. |
| **injection** | SQL (`where("…#{…}")`, raw `execute`), command (`system`, backticks, `Open3`, `exec`, `subprocess(shell=True)`), LDAP, NoSQL, template, XPath, header, log. |
| **xss** | `raw` / `html_safe` / `innerHTML` / `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` / `v-html`; CSP weaknesses (especially `unsafe-inline`, `unsafe-eval`); markdown / HTML sanitiser configuration; user-controlled fields rendered unsanitised. |
| **csrf_redirects_ssrf** | CSRF token verification skips (`skip_before_action :verify_authenticity_token`), open redirects (`redirect_to params[…]`, `allow_other_host: true`), SSRF (URL inputs passed to HTTP clients without allow-listing). |
| **cryptography** | Token comparison with `==` instead of constant-time (`secure_compare`, `crypto.timingSafeEqual`), weak hashes (MD5/SHA1 for passwords), JWT pitfalls (`alg: none`, secret reuse), weak/missing IVs, hard-coded keys. |
| **secrets** | Committed `.env`, hard-coded keys, API keys in code, env-driven `const_get` / `Class.forName`, broad fallbacks (`ENV[…] \|\| 'admin'`), credentials surfaced in logs (incomplete `filter_parameters`). |
| **file_handling** | Path traversal in upload/download, ZIP slip, unrestricted upload types, deserialisation sinks. Check `MiniMagick` / `image_processing` / similar for known issues. |
| **csv_injection** | CSV exports that include user-controlled or admin-controlled fields without escaping leading `= - + @ \t \r`. Check every `CSV.generate` / `csv << […]` / response with `text/csv`. |
| **logging** | Tokens / PII in URLs (referer-leak risk), incomplete `filter_parameters`, log-injection via unescaped newlines, log-format-string bugs. |
| **headers** | CSP, HSTS, host verification (DNS rebinding, `config.hosts`), `X-Frame-Options` / `frame-ancestors`, COOP/COEP if relevant, broad CORS. |
| **rate_limiting** | Missing CAPTCHA / proof-of-work on critical write flows; spoofable IP via untrusted `X-Forwarded-For` (verify trusted-proxy chain); missing rate limits on auth, password reset, signature/feedback endpoints. |
| **misconfiguration** | TLS settings, `assume_ssl`, force-SSL, debug/verbose errors in production, broad CORS, default secrets, `master.key` in git, `config_for(…)` files committed with real values. |

### Search hints (adapt to the stack)

```
grep -rnE "\.where\(\"[^?]*#\{|find_by_sql|connection\.execute|order\(['\"][^?]*#\{" app/ lib/
grep -rnE "system\(|popen|Open3|`|exec\(|eval\(|constantize|YAML\.load[^_]" app/ lib/
grep -rnE "html_safe|raw\(|dangerouslySetInnerHTML|v-html|innerHTML\s*=" app/ src/
grep -rnE "redirect_to.*params\[|allow_other_host" app/ src/
grep -rn  "skip_before_action.*verify_authenticity_token" app/
grep -rnE "==.*token|token.*==" app/controllers/
grep -rn  "before_action.*require_" app/controllers/
```

## Phase 3 — Severity rubric

Assign exactly one severity per finding. Severity captures **impact assuming exploitation succeeds** — not how easy the exploit is. Use these examples to calibrate:

| Severity | Examples |
|---|---|
| **Critical** | Unauthenticated RCE; auth bypass; live secrets in repo; SQL injection in a public endpoint; SAML signature not validated; XSS sink with `unsafe-inline` CSP allowing trivial escalation; privilege escalation via a public flow. |
| **High** | Role check missing on a destructive admin action; CSV/formula injection in widely-shared exports; persistent privilege after offboarding (e.g., SSO removes group but local role retained); stored XSS reachable by all admins; mass-assignment exposing sensitive fields. |
| **Medium** | Token compared with `==` (timing); regex with host-boundary bypass for "trusted" URLs; plaintext HTTP for non-secret data; env-driven `const_get`; cache-invalidation bug in a security control; missing CAPTCHA on a hot abuse path. |
| **Low** | GET-based logout; missing trusted-proxy config; missing `config.hosts` / DNS-rebinding protection; missing CSP nonces on already-protected pages; `enable_starttls_auto: true`; non-standard `Refresh:` header redirect; EOL runtime within 90 days of EOL. |

If a finding doesn't fit cleanly, default downward — better to underclaim than to cry wolf.

## Phase 4 — Exploitability rubric

Assign exactly one exploitability tier per finding. Exploitability captures **how easy it is to actually land the attack** — independent of impact. Answer four questions for each finding:

1. **Who is the attacker?** Anonymous external / any authenticated user / privileged user / network MITM / insider.
2. **What conditions must hold?** None / specific user state / specific config / race window / victim interaction (clickjacking, phishing).
3. **How many steps?** Single request / multi-request chain / chain involving social engineering / chained vulnerabilities.
4. **What capability does the attacker need?** Script-kiddie tooling / intermediate skill / specialist knowledge / nation-state-level resources.

Map answers to one of:

| Exploitability | Pattern | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **Trivial** | Anonymous external attacker, single request, common tooling, no preconditions. | SQL injection in a public search endpoint; CSV-formula injection in a CSV anyone can export; reflected XSS via `?q=…`; missing CAPTCHA on signup. |
| **Moderate** | Requires authentication (any role), multi-step chain, victim interaction (CSRF, phishing), or intermediate skill. | CSRF logout (needs victim to load attacker page); stored XSS only an admin can plant; SSRF reachable only from authenticated UI; race condition with a window of seconds. |
| **Difficult** | Requires privileged access (admin, internal network), narrow race window, theoretical/academic conditions, chained vulnerabilities, or insider trust. | Timing attack on a 117-bit token; privilege-persistence via offboarding (needs offboarding scenario + previously-existing user); RCE only triggered by sysadmin uploading a specific malformed file; bcrypt cost increase via admin config. |

If unsure, default upward (toward Difficult) — overclaiming exploitability creates false urgency.

## Phase 5 — Apply Priority Criteria

Read the **Priority Criteria** block at the bottom of this prompt. Apply filters in this order:

1. **`exclude_categories`** — drop findings in any listed category. Always wins.
2. **`focus_categories`** — if non-empty, drop findings outside the listed categories.
3. **`severity_floor`** — drop findings below this severity. (`low` floor = drop nothing; `critical` floor = drop everything below Critical.)
4. **`min_exploitability`** — drop findings whose exploitability is *lower priority* than this. Priority order is `trivial > moderate > difficult` (Trivial is highest priority; Difficult is lowest). So `min_exploitability: moderate` keeps Trivial and Moderate, drops Difficult. `min_exploitability: trivial` keeps only Trivial.
5. **`project_context`** and **`specific_concerns`** — *do not drop findings*; instead use these to weight severity within surviving findings. A finding that intersects a stated concern can move up one severity tier; a finding that is clearly tangential can move down one tier. Note any tier adjustments in the finding text (e.g., "raised from Medium to High because user flagged SSO offboarding as a specific concern").

Record what was filtered out:

- Categories de-scoped by `focus_categories` / `exclude_categories` → list under "What I did not check."
- Findings dropped by `severity_floor` or `min_exploitability` → mention counts under "What I did not check" (e.g., "5 Low-severity findings and 3 Difficult-exploitability findings omitted by filter").

If filtering results in zero findings, still surface the top 3 most material issues you found regardless of filter, prefixed with a note that they fell outside the user's criteria but are surfaced for awareness.

## Phase 6 — Write `security-review.txt`

Write to `security-review.txt` in the project root, using this exact structure (omit empty severity sections):

```
<repo-name> — Security Review
Date: <YYYY-MM-DD>
Scope: <one-line summary of what was reviewed>
Method: code reading only — no <list of tools/checks you did not run>.
Priority filter applied: <one-line summary, or "none">.

================================================================================
CRITICAL
================================================================================

1. <Short, specific title — name the primitive, not the category>
   Severity: Critical · Exploitability: Trivial
   File: <path:line(s)>

   <code snippet, indented, only if illustrative>

   <Description: what is wrong, in plain prose.>

   <Impact: what an attacker can do, who is affected.>

   <Exploitability rationale: who the attacker is, what conditions are required, how many steps.>

   Fix: <one-paragraph remediation.>

2. <Title>
   ...

================================================================================
HIGH
================================================================================
<…>

================================================================================
MEDIUM
================================================================================
<…>

================================================================================
LOW / DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH
================================================================================
<…>

================================================================================
WHAT I DID NOT CHECK
================================================================================

- <bullet list of explicitly skipped checks>
- <categories de-scoped by the priority filter>
- <count of findings dropped by severity_floor / min_exploitability, if any>

================================================================================
HIGHEST-LEVERAGE NEXT STEPS
================================================================================

1. <single-line action>
2. <single-line action>
3. <single-line action>
```

Number findings sequentially across severities (1, 2, 3, …). Title each finding so a reader can guess the bug from the title alone ("Reviewer role can edit petition content," not "Authorization issue").

